          {
            label: 'DEF CON · Aug 2026',
            content: `<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-100 mb-2">Incident Response 101: Preparing Before the Hack, Responding After It</h3><p><b>DEF CON</b> · Las Vegas, NV · August 2026</p><p class="mt-2">When an organization gets hacked, recovery is only part of the challenge. Security teams must quickly determine what happened, contain the threat, remove the attacker, and restore operations without allowing the incident to happen again. That work falls to incident responders, the cybersecurity professionals who investigate attacks, coordinate response efforts, and help organizations recover under pressure. This beginner-friendly session introduces the role of the incident responder and explains how incident response works across the major stages of preparation, detection, containment, eradication, recovery, and post-incident improvement. Attendees will leave with a practical understanding of what incident responders do, why their work matters, and how structured response processes help organizations reduce damage and improve resilience after a cyber incident.</p><p class="mt-1"><a class="text-primary underline hover:underline-offset-2" target="_blank" href="https://sessionize.com/s/joshua-morgan/incident-response-101-preparing-before-the-hack-re/184125">View Sessionize Session</a></p>`,
          },
          {
            label: 'DEF CON · Aug 2025',
            content: `<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-100 mb-2">Incident Response 101: What Happens After the Hack?</h3><p><b>DEF CON</b> · Las Vegas, NV · August 2025</p><p class="mt-2">Ever wonder what happens behind the scenes when a company gets hacked? Restoring systems, containing the damage, and keeping attackers out for good doesn't happen by magic — it takes skilled professionals to guide the process. Enter the Incident Responder: part digital detective, part crisis manager. Their job is to figure out what went wrong, kick out the bad actors, and make sure it doesn't happen again. Join us for a beginner-friendly presentation on the essential role of Incident Responders in cybersecurity.</p><p class="mt-1"><a class="text-primary underline hover:underline-offset-2" target="_blank" href="https://sessionize.com/s/joshua-morgan/incident-response-101-what-happens-after-the-hack/181148">View Sessionize Session</a></p>`,
          },
          {
            label: 'BSides · Mar 2025',
            content: `<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-100 mb-2">From RTFM to BTFM: Helping Shape New Beginnings</h3><p><b>BSides San Diego</b> · San Diego, CA · March 2025</p><p class="mt-2">In the Information Security ecosystem, stereotypes for practitioners persist, and they're not always the most flattering. If we approached these stereotypes like we do our technical work, perhaps we could do better for ourselves and others. What can we do to help dispel the stereotypes and can we use them to our advantage? For those of us who have been in the industry for years, we may be used to how things are or how 'they used to be', but we sometimes miss how what we do affects those looking in from the outside wanting to break in. I share how I was affected when I first moved into the information security world, lessons I've learned in my time within the industry, and ways to make small improvements to pave a better path for those to come after us.</p><p class="mt-1"><a class="text-primary underline hover:underline-offset-2" target="_blank" href="https://sessionize.com/s/joshua-morgan/from-rtfm-to-btfm-helping-shape-new-beginnings/181147">View Sessionize Session</a></p>`,
